<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klemens von Metternich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Prince Metternich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Klemens von Metternich was Austrian foreign minister from 1809 and chancellor from 1821 until 1848. A leading figure at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1814–1815, he shaped a conservative European order based on the balance of power and cooperation among monarchies. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the German Confederation he suppressed liberal and national movements through censorship and police, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> forced him to flee.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich.jpg" alt="Klemens von Metternich" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Metternich_by_Lawrence.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Public life under <b>Metternich</b> was constrained by censorship, so the comfortable private culture of the Biedermeier middle classes flourished instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>German patriots had hoped for a national state, but Austria under Prince <b>Klemens von Metternich</b>, the middle states such as Bavaria and Württemberg, and the great powers all preferred a league that would preserve the sovereignty of its members and the balance of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Klemens von Metternich</b> built the monarchist Conservative Order of the Congress of Vienna to balance power in Europe and suppress republican and nationalist movements.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was mainly an administrative label: it was divided into two governorates, at Laibach and Trieste, each run by Austrian officials under the system of <b>Metternich</b>, with provincial estates of little power.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-illyria">Kingdom of Illyria</a></li></ul></section>
